  ###  ###            ###  ### YLWEEN      - An RPG in the TRUE SPIRIT -

                 Watch out Hack'n'Slash - Here we come!

  =-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-

               'Time stands still in these ancient halls
                only the castle itself can tell what it keeps.
                Dark are the secrets between these walls,
                hidden in shadows of death while the sorceror sleeps.'
                                            - Candlemass...

----

    There is a realm very far away, not necessarily on our world, called
Rylween.  Rylween is one of the seven kingdoms of this world.  It lies in
the northwestern part of this world's only known continent, surrounded by
impenetratable mountains in all the directions.  Rumor has it, that there
is a canyon somewhere in the mountains to the south, but no one has ever 
found it.  Rylween is an isolated kingdom, whose good and justful monarch
went by the name Zalkin Ammol. 

    One day, a lone knight rode into the capital of Rylween, Wilor.  He 
approached from the south and was dressed in purple rags.  He demanded
to see Ammol, and when they met in Ammol's palace, the knight pulled
a trident and pierced Ammol with it before anyone had time to react.
When the guards tried to attack the knight, they found him invulnerable
to normal weapons.  He just stood there, 7 feet tall in his purple robe
and laughed.  He waved his hands and disappeared in a puff of smoke.
Before he was gone the royal guard heard him yell:

   'I declare war. This shall be the last part of the realm to fall.
 For I am Baron Ydarg, master of the seven kingdoms, the new ruler of
 Rylween.'

   He built a stronghold somewhere to the southwest, the evil castle Zadraz,
in one night.  The following weeks where chaotic.  Strange and evil creatures
started to appear in the realm, and many good merchant was slain on his trips
through the forests.  All citizens of Rylween where panicstricken.  The 
warrior guilds, in the four cities of Rylween, started to search for veteran
fighters, fit for protecting the realm.  They failed.

This is where you come in.

----

   Rylween is a role playing online game with no player interaction.  There
is, however, a top list where you can see how well the different players
succeed. Your final goal is to hunt down Baron Ydarg and destroy him (but 
he's immune to magic and quite nasty) - this is where the game will end.
Rylween will also create a hall of fame file with all the winners. 

  All positions of the different dungeons/people/huts etc. are randomized 
each time a new campaign begins.  Thus it isn't the same game each time.
The missions are the same, but the positions of all the important locations
always vary.  Good, eh? I thought quite a lot about how to make an 
adventure/RPG online interesting more than once, and this is what I found
to be the best method. 

----

  When the game starts you will be at the warrior guild menu. This is where
you create adventurer parties, continue previously saved games, delete 
parties you aren't satisfied with and so on.  If you are a new player - 
create a new party here.

  You will direct a party of four heroes through Rylween.  This is where you
create them.  First, enter a name for your party.  This name will be used on
the top list, and should be something fantasy-ish.

  Now it's time to create the first character.  You will see his four basic
attributes, and can choose between rerolling them or keeping them.  You can
reroll them an infinite number of times, but the initial values can never be
greater than 75 for any skill.

  Weapon Skill:     
  =-=-=-=-=-=-=
  This is how good the player is in combat.  Fighters need a weapon skill
  greater than 50.

  Health:
  =-=-=-=
  This is this character's hit point maximum.  Each time he is wounded
  his hit points decrease. If they drop below 0 the characted will die.
  A character can never get more hit points than his health, though.

  Wisdom:
  =-=-=-=
  This determines how skilled the character is in the arts of Magick.
  His magic point maximum is equal to his wisdom value.  Only priests
  and mages get magic points, though.  Wisdom is of no importance whatsoever
  to any of the other classes.  Mages and priests need a wisdom value
  greater than 50.

  Charisma:
  =-=-=-=-=
  This measures the characters general personality and leadership skills.
  The party will gain more friends and less enemies if they have many 
  characters with high charisma. Bards need a charisma value greater
  than 50.

Now, when you have decided you want to keep some good values, enter a name
for this character. Once again, names with fantasy touch will create a 
better atmosphere during the game.

Select a race for this character:

Human
-=-=-
Well.  I hope I don't need to explain this one. (except maybe for Alex May).

Dwarf
-=-=-
Small stout bearded humanoids.  They make excellent fighters.

Elf
-=-
Tall slender humanoids.  Excellent rangers or maybe mages.

Halflings
-=-=-=-=-
Short humanoids with hairy feet.  Excellent thieves, and their fighting
skill is not to be underestimated.

Next, pick a class for the character:

Fighter
-=-=-=-
Warriors.  High level fighter veterans are fearsome opponents.  Especially
when you happen to encounter a creature immune to magic. A level 20
fighter with a good weapon can easily kill a man with one blow.  Fighters
have no magic skill.

Thief
-=-=-
Rouges.  Experts in disarming traps and picking locks.  Good to have in
your party when exploring dungeons and caves.  Thieves have no magic skill.

Mage
-=-=
Wizards.  Mages are often physically weak but they are experts in 
spellcasting.  Mage spells are offensive and can only be used in battle.

Priest
-=-=-=
Priests are servants under one of Rylween's many gods.  They also know
magic (but not the same kind as mages).  Their spells are defensive and
can only be used when not in combat.

Bard
=-=-
Bards are experts in telling stories and singing traveller's tunes.  A bard
is also often quite a fearsome fighter.  Having a bard in your party will
make you popular in Rylween's many taverns.  Bards have no magic skill.

Ranger
-=-=-=
Lonely hunters.  Rangers are almost as good warriors as fighters.  They can
sometimes trap wild animals for the party to eat.  Rangers have no magic
skill.

Your initial game will now be saved. Select 'continue saved game' from
the guild menu.

------------

The map screen will now show an overview of the surrounding countryside.
You are on the highlighted square in the middle, and the terrain above
is to the north, the terrain below to the south and so on. The different
terrain types are:

/\    =  Mountains. High impenetratable peaks.
^^    =  Foothills.
~~    =  Water. Impenetratable.
gg    =  Forests.
..    =  Plainland.
%%    =  Swamps.
::    =  Deserts.
TT    =  Towne.
HH    =  Hut.
CC    =  Castle (there is only one castle, and I wouldn't enter it that
         soon if I were you).

Pressing '?' will bring up a control summary. Here is a more verbose
explanation:

C = Camp

You can be awake for about 12 hours without loss of health, but even the 
most experienced adventurer needs to sleep.  You'll see how it works.  A 
rule of thumb is to always post a guard.  There is always a risk of being
ambushed in your sleep.  If you DESPERATELY need the sleep, don't post a
guard.  The nights of Rylween can be very cold.  Buy some suitable equipment
in the nearest town as soon as possible so that you won't freeze to death.
(I was inspired by the Swedish weather right now).

E = Enter

Enter a town, castle or hut.  You need to be standing on a square of that
kind for this command to work.

H = Hunt

If you have a ranger in your party, he might trap a rabbit or some other
animal for you to eat.  This is good if you are starving and out of food.

J = View Journal

View the traveller's journal where all important coordinates and such
are recorded, so that you don't need to write them down. Nice one.

L = Leave Item

Discard an item in the party inventory.  You'll never see it alive again.

P = Put Down Weapon

Just unready a weapon you are wielding.

Q = Quaff Potion

There are different kinds of potions, I won't tell you what they do, but
the merchant will sell you an average working healing brew.  Beware of
strange coloured liquids, but do experiment. (Save your game first).
There is no low-level-format-potion.  Your sysop will rejoice when he hears
that.

R = Ready Armour

If you have an armour in the party inventory and want one of your 
characters to wear it, select the armour and he will put it on.  If he 
already was wearing an armour, he will take that one off and it will replace
the new armour's position in the inventory.

S = Save Game

DO THIS OFTEN!  You have one save position.  Save your game after you have
found some new equipment.  Save your game before entering towns.  Save your
game after leaving towns.  Save your game before entering that house with
impaled human skulls outside.  Save your game whenever it seems appropriate.
You can always go back to your last saved game if you are killed.

T = Take Off Armour

Take off an armour you are wearing.

V = View Party

A status sheet for a special party member or all of them if you want.  You
will also see some general info about the party.

W = Wield Weapon

See wield armour.  It's the same, but the active objects are weapons.

X = Quit Game (No Save)

Save before doing this.  This will take you back to the board.  Good if you
were just robbed of all your belongings.  Just exit and restore the last save.

Z = Cast Spell

Priest magic.  Only the priest knows about his art.  He wouldn't tell me :)
- But do experiment.  I don't want to reveal all.

8 = Move North

4 = Move West

6 = Move East

2 = Move South

! = Redraw Screen

------

You will start the game near the northwestern part of Rylween.  The first
thing you should do is to visit the mountain village Thord and buy some arms.
You are completely defenseless when the game starts.

There are four towns in Rylween:

Thord - City of the Mountains
-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-
In the mountain range to the northwest.  I shall be generous and reveal its
coordinates, 12x11.

Ollk - City of the Swamp
-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=
Ollk lies in the Great Swamp in the northeastern part of the realm.

Wilor - City of the Forest
-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=
Wilor is the capital of Rylween.  Wilor lies in the center of the realm, in
the large forest Lonewood.

Murn - City of the Desert
-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-
Murn lies somewhere in the southeastern desert.  Not many travellers have
made it to this part of the land. It is about as far away as you can get
from Thord.

People in the taverns might tell you where the towns are more exact.

--------------

The Status Bar:
-=-=-=-=-=-=-=

That is the green thingy at the bottom of the screen.

TIME: What time of the day it is.

ST:   Party Status.  OK, Hungry or Starving.  When you are starving,
      it is wise to let your ranger (if you have one) trap something
      to eat, or rush to a town to get some food rations at the merchant's.

X:    X coordinate.

Y:    Y coordinate.

FD:   How many food rations you've got left.

MV:   Moves left today.

AU:   (Aurum) - How much gold you have.

Below these values are the hit point statistics for the characters of the
party. 0 = dead.

--------------

Townes :
-=-=-=-

You can visit all the institutions in a town. Here is a small explanation.

SMITH
-=-=-
The smith is a nice chap. You can buy or sell armours and weapons here.
Sometimes he might even invite you for a beer.  It is always wise to accept.

TAVERN
-=-=-=
The tavern is always wise to visit.  You can buy refreshments, listen to
rumors, raise your reputation by telling stories and most important, talk to
people.  They may grant you missions or give you important information.
Talk to everyone you meet!  This is the way to success.

INN
-=-
Good to stay at if you are tired.  You will restore more hitpoints than
if you sleep in the wilderness, you won't be murdered by orcs in the
middle of the night, and you can have a nice warm supper here.  The Inn
also houses many important characters you should talk to.

MERCHANT
-=-=-=-=
Convenience store.  Here, you can buy things like blankets, food rations,
torches and so on.  You can also sell any odd items you find, but the
merchant won't let you haggle.  The smith is a much nicer guy.

CHURCH
-=-=-=
There are only two churches.  In Thord and in Wilor.  You can heal
wounded characters here, or raise dead ones.  For a price.

Some of the institutions are not open 24 hours. Please respect this, and
keep an eye on the status bar.

----

Battles
-=-=-=-

Battles play an important role in this realm. You may be ambushed
when trying to rest in the wilderness. You may be attacked without
warning when exploring caves. Beasts and bandits are all around,
and they are after YOU!

When you encounter one or more creatures, you can choose between
fighting and running. It is not always you get away at the first battle
round. Then the actual battle starts. Each player can do a certain
number of things. Here is an explanation:

Attack

Just strike at the enemy with your readied weapon.

Defend

Rest a round and recover a bit, while the other guys do the fighting.

Use Item

Use a magical item of yours. The feared Kael's Axe is the most powerful
magic weapon in the realm. Try finding at least ONE Kael's Axe before
entering castle Zadraz.

Battle Cry

Try to scare your enemies.
It works best on yellow creatures like kobolds, goblins etc.

Cast Spell

Only for mages. The mage can cast one of the six battle spells:
  
  Frost Finger - Strike an enemy with a bolt of pure cold.
  Fire Cloud   - Engulf all the enemies in a cloud of burning air.
  Confusion    - Will make the party start to waver and blur in front
                 of the enemy, making you more difficult to hit.
  Death Strike - KILL! One enemy.
  Hold Enemy   - Hold the enemy party so that you get an extra battle
                 round.
  Anti Magic   - Create a magic shield around your foes so that they
                 can't use magic during the next attack round. 
                 (Good against wizards and foes of magical origin).
  
The battle is over when all the enemies are dead, or you are dead, or
when you have fled.

Experience points and gold are given to you if you win the battle.

----

Dungeons and Caves
-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=

There are lots of dungeons and caves scattered throughout the realm.
You may stumble upon them by chance or you may know where to go.
Dungeons play an important role in the different missions you can receive.
A dungeon/cave consists of an unknown number of levels. To enter the
next level, choose D)escend from the dungeon menu. To return to
the previous level, choose A)scend. Dungeons are full of all kinds
of nice monsters that don't exist up in the wilderness.
You can use all the standard wilderness commands in the dungeon.

----

Missions
-=-=-=-=

The missions are the heart of this game. It is quite meaningless just
to roam the land for fame and treasure (although that also is a possibility).
Try to solve the quests you are granted. Therefore, it is always wise
to talk to all the people you meet at taverns/inns or in the wilderness.
Some have important clues, some grant you missions, some are not
important at all. Each missions is a "trail" you must follow.

HINT: If you want some quick experience and money, try the first mission.
      Go to THORD, City of the mountains and talk to Garth, who resides
      in this city in ALL the campaigns. He will grant you a simple
      starting quest.

----

Attitudes
-=-=-=-=-

Just like in real life, you can pick your path. Here are a few suggestions:

Bounty Hunters

Your party roams the wilderness, searching for fame and fortune.
The only goal is to get as mighty as possible.

Freedom Force

Your party does its very utmost to assasinate Baron Ydarg, the evil
ruler of Rylween. This is the main purpose of the game, but you don't
NEED to kill him if you don't want to.
When Ydarg dies, the campaign will restart, and the victorious party
will enter the Hall of Fame.

Neutral

Just be neutral. Don't cooperate with anyone special. Be yourselves.

The Power Items
-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-

To kill the evil Baron Ydarg, you will need a couple of magic items.
The five power items are: The Master Crown (the most powerful object
in the realm), The Fire Shield, The Foot of Jazzy, The Silver Key and
the Bracelets of Thun. When you have accuired all of these through
different missions, you are ready to find Ydarg's castle Zadraz and
breach its defenses...

----
